//! Caravan beckon -- a plan carries the wall it stood out to the person who
//! can act on it, rather than keeping the ending in a drawer only the plan
//! opens.
//!
//! `desist.rye` opened the last door: a matter that has come round twice meets
//! a boundary rather than a third lap, and the wall stands where the
//! correspondence used to run.
//!
//! **And then it stands there, in the plan's own notes.** Every note of this
//! arc lands under `note_dir`, where an operator learns of it by coming to
//! look. So a correspondence exhausts itself honestly and privately: the loop
//! ends, the record is exact, and the one person who could take the matter up
//! outside the plan is never told it stopped.
//!
//! **The measurement is walls a plan stood and told nobody about.** The rung
//! below ends the correspondence going round forever; this one ends the ending
//! being kept where only the plan reads.
//!
//! **Beckoning is carrying, and carrying is a record.** A run that beckons
//! writes the eight bytes of the call into the reader's own box, beside every
//! other word this arc has carried them, and reads that box back before it
//! believes anybody was reached.
//!
//! **A run calls a person only to a wall it has already stood.** A call
//! published by a run that bounded nothing would summon a reader to a
//! correspondence still running. `BeckonUnwalled` refuses by name.
//!
//! **A call lands where a person actually is.** A reader who left no address
//! has no box to open, and a call written into the air reaches nobody while
//! reporting that somebody was reached. `BeckonUnaddressed` refuses by name.
//!
//! **The call names the wall it carries.** A reader's box may still hold a call
//! left by a run before today, and reading that as this one's would report a
//! person reached about a boundary nobody stood here. `BeckonMismatched` is the
//! rung itself.
//!
//! **The wall stands before the call goes out.** The boundary lands first and
//! the summons after, so a run falling between the two leaves a wall standing
//! and uncarried -- exactly the state the rung below always left -- rather than
//! calling a person to a boundary that does not exist.
//!
//! **Beckoning is not settling.** The run says a bounded matter is worth a
//! person's attention and rewrites nothing else: the settlement, the quarrel,
//! its age, its standing, the case on the desk, the finding, the plan's own
//! word, the concession, the meeting, the redress, the delivery, the reader's
//! first verdict, the second look, the telling, the reader's reply, the mark,
//! and the wall itself each come out of a beckoning run exactly as they went in
//! -- and both runs settle `carried out whole`.
//!
//! Five refusals of this rung's own, matching the five below it.
//! `BeckonUnwalled` is the moment a call is carried at all: a run that stood no
//! wall has no boundary to call anyone to. `BeckonUnaddressed` is the same
//! guard one step in -- a reader with no address has no box a run may reach.
//! `BeckonMismatched` is the whole point. `BeckonMiscounted` is the honesty of
//! the count: every stood wall is carried or kept, never both and never
//! neither. `BeckonMisrecorded` is the record, read back out of the reader's
//! own box rather than out of the memory of having written to it.
//!
//! **A call survives a provisioning, and outlives the drawer the wall it names
//! was kept in** -- proven as its own fold, since a summons swept every morning
//! would leave the person who can act on the matter exactly as uncalled as
//! before.
//!
//! Usage:
//!   beckon            Run the self-test (default)
//!   beckon selftest   Same as default
//!   beckon dependent ...  Run as one supervised dependent (spawned by the run)

